> Access to Innovative Medicines Tip SheetDeveloped by the Preventive Cardiovascular Nurses Association, this is a critical reference includes tips and resources for how to navigate administrative systems, maximize efficiency of the prior authorization process, and[ improve] nurse[ job satisfaction] and[ adherence]/patient[ outcomes]. Particularly focused on PCSK9, NOAC and new heart failure therapies.
Impro[ving] Med[ication] Ad[herence] in Cardiometabolic Disease Practical and Regulatory ImplicationsThis paper[ summarizes] the scope of CVD nonad[herence],[ describes key] U.S. Food and Drug Administration initiatives, and identifies potential targets for[ improvement].
Grand rounds – Overcoming Barriers to Medication Adherence for Chronic Diseases (CDC) Speakers discuss[ research], interventions, education, and emerging tools and technologies[ that] may help overcome[ barriers] to[ medication] non-ad[herence].
ABC Prior Authorization Resource Kits The Prior Authorization Work Group for the ABC Access to Care initiative has developed resources to help cardiology practices better respond to the administrative needs of the current prior authorization process and[ improve patient] access to appropriate,[ evidence-]based therapies. These Resource Kits have been uniquely designed for cardiology practices working with underserved minority patients. The Resource Kits have also been constructed to serve physicians, nurses, and other staff members that interact with PA.
PCNA Heart Healthy Toolbox This extensive resource includes background[ information] and strategies for[ professionals], plus focused[ sheets] to share with patients regarding healthy eating, exercise and total health.
PCNA Access to Innovative Medicines: Professional and Patient Resources Innovative therapies to prevent and treat heart disease and stroke fill an important health care[ need] for many. The resources for health[ care] providers both providers and their[ patients] focus[ on strategies] and tips to help patients get the medication they need—and to adhere to prescribed therapies to improve[ patient outcomes].
Cardiologist’s Perceptions of Access to New Therapies 2016 survey (ACC)Revealed[ that] implementing new[ therapies] is a problem[,] requiring more professional time due to administrative barriers[;] The top two[ barriers] to trying new[ therapies] are a) cost and b) prior authorization documentation[;] Cardiologists feel very favorably about prescribing new medications[.] And[,] cardiologists would be much more likely to prescribe appropriate new[ therapies] if they did not experience delays/denials.
Provider survey on challenges in prescribing PCSK9 (NLA)A[ healthcare] provider’s compet[ency] in dyslipidemia[ management] requires a thorough understanding of existing and emerging[ evidence]-[based therapies that] they can utilize in clinical[ practice.] Watch these short[ videos] and learn about the NLA

